{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261112T013501Z-1bf47a7f", "ts": "2026-11-12T01:40:26Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "feature/review-round-cost", "build": "9e93e8bdf2b4"}, "body": {"base_commit": "9e93e8bdf2b49b74c9123fcad6de1e06bbf188d1", "base_tree": "02815e8ff12356c937a6cd197b5be870acf9b592", "head_tree": "de21bba0e52d5b6dcdfc24bd009c5ae8e55aee36", "head_commit": "2ca74334ee911e1d9d209689537b47a52ba508e9", "dispatch_commit": "2ca74334ee911e1d9d209689537b47a52ba508e9", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/issues/847-design.md", "plugin/agents/critic-reviewer.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/coordinator.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_test_run_facts.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-review-round-cost.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/review-round-cost.md", "documentation/issues/847-design.md", "plugin/agents/critic-reviewer.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/coordinator.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_test_run_facts.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-review-round-cost.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/review-round-cost.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Inherited-baseline waiver fails open when the merge-base is the reviewed work itself (HEAD on the base branch, or the HEAD~1 fallback base)", "recommendation": "`evidence.inherited_failures` takes `git merge-base HEAD <base>` as 'the tree before this work' and never checks that it is. Two reachable cases break that. (1) Work committed on the base branch itself with no remote ahead of it (e.g. base `main`, HEAD on `main`, which project-preferences may allow): merge-base == HEAD, so the 'base tree' is HEAD's own tree, and the builder's own red `test-evidence record` on that tree is the 'base run'. Every failing id then matches and consolidate waives every test-failure BLOCKING the work caused. (2) A repo where `_resolve_base_branch` falls through to the `HEAD~1` candidate (`_DEFAULT_BASE_CANDIDATES`, e.g. a `master` repo with no origin and no `base_branch:`): merge-base == HEAD~1, so a failure the branch introduced one commit earlier and recorded there is waived as 'inherited'. `cmd_test_evidence` already warns that HEAD~1 is a moving ref and not a base. Either way the gates (`resolution_index` reads `waived` as resolving) pass a branch-caused red suite with no fix and no verify round. That makes the plan's disposition 'authority fails closed; advice fails soft -> conforms; ... the waiver fails closed when no base-tree run lists every id' untrue of the diff for this path. Fix (class: the code assumes merge-base means 'before this work' without checking it): in `inherited_failures`, return `(None, set())` when the merge-base commit equals HEAD, when the base tree equals the reviewed `manifest['head_tree']`, and when the base resolved to the `HEAD~1` fallback. Add red-first tests for each case, alongside the existing no-waiver cases in `TestAnInheritedFailureIsWaivedByConsolidate`, which only ever builds a real `feat/work` branch off `main`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The waiver trusts the reviewer's id list against an evidence record capped at 100 ids, so a hidden new failure can ride a waiver", "recommendation": "The plan's constraint for #900 is 'the cap of 100 ids fails closed'. Only the base side does: an id the base run's cap dropped never matches. On the reviewed side, `.test-evidence.json` `failed_tests` is capped at 100 too (`_FAILED_TESTS_CAP`), and the reviewer is told to copy the ids from there. Take a branch with failed=120 whose first 100 visible ids all appear in the base run. The finding names those 100, they all match, and the waiver fires, even though the 20 hidden failures can include new ones the branch caused. Nothing in `_waive_inherited_failures` compares the finding's ids with the reviewed tree's own run, so a reviewer that lists only the inherited subset of a mixed failure set also gets the whole finding waived. Fix (instance): read the newest non-narrowed `test-run` on `manifest['head_tree']` and refuse the waiver when its `failed` exceeds `len(failed_tests)` (capped) or when there is no such run. The construction that also closes the subset case: waive only when every failing id of the head-tree run is either in the base set or named by a BLOCKING finding of this review that is not waived. Pin the capped case with a test.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "derive_branch_cache renders a waived review without its waivers, so an ingesting clone is told to fix what was waived", "recommendation": "Only `consolidate` passes `waived=` to `fact_to_cache_record`/`next_action_line`. `derive_branch_cache` (the ingest path, which renders 'as consolidation would') calls `fact_to_cache_record(fact, carried=carried)`. The waiver resolution facts do travel to that clone on the review's note, but in that clone the cache says 'Blocking findings must be resolved before proceeding.' and the NEXT-ACTION line opens with '1 BLOCKING ...' and asks for a fix. The builder then buys the verify round #900 exists to remove. The gates are unaffected, because they read facts. It broke because the waived set is a parameter the one writer computes, not something the renderer derives from the store. Class fix: have `fact_to_cache_record` derive the waived fids itself from the store's `resolution` facts with `origin: inherited-baseline` targeting `fact['id']`, so every renderer agrees, and drop the parameter. Add a test that runs `derive_branch_cache` over a store holding a waiver.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "The waiver's fail-closed error paths are untested", "recommendation": "Two fail-closed claims rest on branches no test reaches. (a) `_waive_inherited_failures` returns None when `append_fact` fails, and `consolidate` then exits 1 with the partials kept, which its docstring says 'a retry heals'. (b) `inherited_failures` returns `(None, set())` on a schema-ahead `test-run` fact and on an unreadable store. The new tests cover only the happy path and the no-match cases. Per 'A check never seen to return non-zero proves nothing', add one test that forces the append to fail and asserts exit 1, no waiver and the partials still on disk, followed by a retry that heals. Add one with a schema-ahead `test-run` line asserting no waiver.", "files": ["tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Waived state is passed in as a parameter, not read from the facts, so two renders of one review fact disagree", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: `fact_to_cache_record` and `next_action_line` learn which findings are waived from a `waived` argument that only `consolidate` passes. They do not derive it from the resolution facts the gates read (`coverage_algebra.resolution_index`). `derive_branch_cache` renders the same fact with `fact_to_cache_record(fact, carried=carried)`. That is the path a clone takes when it ingests the review through notes, and it has no `waived`. So in that clone the cache says 'Blocking findings must be resolved before proceeding' and NEXT-ACTION asks for a fix or verify round on a finding the gate already treats as resolved. That is the round the waiver exists to save. The per-finding entries also carry no waived marker, so a later verify reviewer reading the 'prior findings record' cannot tell a waived blocker from an open one. Meanwhile the new `origin`/`base_run` fields have no code reader at all. The core rule applies: 'A channel that is produced and never consumed is a DEFECT'. The fix is a construction, not a third call site passing the list. Have the renderer compute the waived fids from the store: resolution facts whose `finding.review_id` is the fact's id and whose `origin == INHERITED_BASELINE_ORIGIN`. Give that one owner, called by both `consolidate` and `derive_branch_cache`. Mark each waived entry in the cache's `findings`, which also gives `origin` its consumer. Bound of the class: every caller of `fact_to_cache_record` and `next_action_line`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "evidence.inherited_failures re-implements coverage.resolve_merge_base_tree through a private import, and puts base-branch policy inside the store module", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the classifier resolves the base, the merge-base and `^{tree}` itself. It calls `coverage._resolve_base_branch` (private) and `run_git` directly. `coverage.resolve_merge_base_tree` already owns exactly this prelude, and its docstring says it exists because three divergent copies of it were the drift class it killed. The copy also makes `evidence.py`, the fact-store layer, depend upward on `coverage` through a lazy import. Change `inherited_failures(project_dir, base_tree, test_ids)` to take the tree, and have `_waive_inherited_failures` resolve it with `coverage.resolve_merge_base_tree`. The store module then stays a reader of facts, and the waiver fails closed on the same `step` errors every other merge-base consumer sees. To check for other copies, grep `run_git(.*\"merge-base\"` outside `coverage.py`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Only goals-1-3.md tells a reviewer to fill failed_tests, so the waiver cannot fire on a final or cumulative review", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: the producer half of #900 went into the protocol file for `chunk`/`verify-resolutions` only. `review-cycle.md` says consolidation waives in 'any mode', and the change record says 'the branch's next review waives' the finding. But the `final`/`cumulative` protocol has no such instruction. That protocol is read by every roster reviewer, and by the PR boundary's own cumulative review. Its Goal 1 still reads 'Test failures in evidence \u2192 **BLOCKING**.' with no `failed_tests`. `critic-reviewer.md`'s partial schema, which says 'match it exactly', omits the field, so a careful reviewer reads it as forbidden. On the review where an inherited red base costs the most, the correctness reviewer never names the ids, and the builder pays the verify round. This is the authoring rule 'When you add a rule at the site that motivated it, ENUMERATE the siblings performing the same ACT'. The act is 'a reviewer rates a test failure', and it lives in `review-protocol.md` Goal 1 and in both partial-schema blocks (`critic-reviewer.md` and `goals-1-3.md`). Add the clause and the optional `failed_tests?` field at each of them. Better, pin it with a test asserting that every file defining the partial finding schema names `failed_tests`, so the next schema field cannot skip a surface.", "files": ["plugin/agents/critic-reviewer.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The one-reviewer remedy is written to actors that never meet the refusal, while the surfaces that do meet it still say re-run the review", "recommendation": "Class: every surface that tells someone what to do after a refused consolidation. Why it broke: the remedy prose went to the dispatcher's files, not to whoever is present when the refusal surfaces.\n\n- `coordinator.md` step 3 tells the coordinator to re-dispatch the failing reviewer. The same step tells it to stop and never resume, so it cannot observe a refusal.\n- `SKILL.md`'s new sentence tells the fork to re-dispatch that `critic-reviewer`. The only refusal a fork ever meets is from its own inline single-pass consolidate, where `_partial_failure_message` correctly says to rewrite the partial and re-run. The same paragraph also says 'report the refusal's stderr and stop'. Those are three contradictory instructions for one case.\n- The surfaces that actually reach the main session were not updated. `building.md` ('If it fails, tell the user and re-invoke') is two lines from a line this diff edits. The Stop backstop blocker in `prawduct-hook` says 'Follow its remedy, or re-run the review: /prawduct:critic'. Both route to `critic-begin`, which archives the sound partials. A SubagentStop refusal exits 0, so its stderr is not what the model reads.\n\nThe core rule applies: 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees'. Fix: in `SKILL.md`, state the inline remedy (rewrite your partial, re-run consolidate). Drop the coordinator sentence, or say the refusal is the main session's to act on. In `building.md` and the Stop blocker, defer to the refusal's own named remedy rather than offering a whole re-run beside it.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/coordinator.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "scope-trace: the inherited-baseline waiver cannot fire at final or cumulative, because no reviewer there is told to write failed_tests", "recommendation": "Instance. The waiver needs a BLOCKING finding to carry a non-empty `failed_tests` (`_waive_inherited_failures` drops every candidate without one). The only instruction to write that field is in goals-1-3.md Goal 1, and that file is read only by `chunk` and `verify-resolutions`. At `final`/`cumulative`, reviewers follow review-protocol.md, and its Goal 1 line still reads 'Test failures in evidence -> BLOCKING' with no `failed_tests`. The partial schema in critic-reviewer.md, which every coordinator-roster reviewer writes against, does not list the field either. So the cumulative review that feeds the PR gate rates an inherited failure BLOCKING with no ids, no waiver is written, and the builder pays the verify-resolutions round #900 was meant to remove, at the boundary where it matters most. The shipped change record's Consumer note says 'the branch's next review waives a BLOCKING finding for that same failure itself', which is false whenever that next review is final or cumulative. The fix is to add the `failed_tests` clause to review-protocol.md Goal 1 and the field to critic-reviewer.md's partial schema, then pin it with a payload test like the existing goals-1-3 one, so every protocol file that rates test failures carries the field.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/agents/critic-reviewer.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", ".prawduct/changes/review-round-cost.md"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A waiver that does not fire says nothing about why", "recommendation": "Instance (one classifier, one caller). `evidence.inherited_failures` has six fail-closed exits that all return `(None, set())` with no reason: no base resolved, no merge-base, no tree, store unreadable, a schema-ahead test-run, or no non-narrowed run on that tree. `_waive_inherited_failures` then `continue`s silently, both on those exits and when some id is missing from the base set (a newer green run, or the 100-id cap). A builder who followed the Consumer note or building.md's new step and recorded a base run still sees an ordinary BLOCKING finding and a fix instruction. Nothing tells them the run met the wrong tree (for example, a different `<base>` than the one `coverage._resolve_base_branch` picks), was skipped as narrowed, or was superseded. So they re-record blindly or pay the verify round. The fix is to have the classifier return a reason with its None. Consolidate should then print one stderr line, alongside the existing 'waived ...' line, for each BLOCKING finding that carries `failed_tests` and was not waived. That line names the cause and the base tree it looked for.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The resolution's new origin field has no reader, and the resolution consumers count an inherited waiver as a judged one", "recommendation": "Instance. Per the rule 'A channel that is produced and never consumed is a DEFECT', the field needs a reader. `origin: inherited-baseline` and `base_run` are written on the resolution fact, but nothing in plugin/lib reads `origin` (grep: zero hits outside critic_consolidate.py). The existing resolution consumers were not shown to have been weighed: the plan's governed_by dispositions cover the findings cache only. `dispositions.resolution_detail_index` and `finding_state` map the waiver to plain STATE_WAIVED. `stats.finding_outcome` therefore reports it in `review-stats` as 'waived', the same bucket as a verify pass's judged waiver, which mixes 'a reviewer accepted a blocker' with 'the base was already red' in the yield figures this repo measures from. `finding_state`'s docstring still justifies precedence because a resolution 'is the only answer an independent verify pass attests', which is now false for these facts. The fix is to either give the origin a reader (a distinct state or outcome, for example `waived-inherited`, through `resolution_detail_index`) or record a decision that the census and stats deliberately treat them as waived and fix the docstring. Either way the resolution consumers get named in the change.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md"], "fid": "R-11"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 10, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "merge_findings dedup can drop a duplicate's failed_tests", "recommendation": "`merge_findings` keeps the first entry when two findings with the same `(goal, name, files)` key have equal severity, so the second one's `failed_tests` are lost. That is the 'shrink the set the waiver must cover' outcome the `validate_partial` comment refuses bad elements to prevent. Rare in practice, because one role owns Goal 1. Unioning `failed_tests` on a merge would close it.", "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: remaining dispositions hold", "recommendation": "I checked every recorded `governed_by:` disposition against the diff. The exit-code contract (0/1/2, including the single-positional guard), one `check_partial` behind both callers, one classifier, additive fact fields, the narrowed-run skip, deterministic waiver ids with an idempotent replay, and the data-model amendment citing the 2026-10-21 owner ruling with a veto marker are all true as stated. 'Authority fails closed' is untrue for the merge-base == HEAD/HEAD~1 path (first finding). 'The findings cache shows waived findings' holds only on the consolidate path (third finding). No other departure.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The inline-fork predicate is re-derived in _partial_failure_message", "recommendation": "`roster == [\"reviewer\"] and not manifest.get(\"reviewer_model\")` restates a distinction the module already computes elsewhere (`handed_off=bool(manifest.get(\"reviewer_model\"))` for the incomplete-roster message). It is borderline at two sites. If a third appears, name it once, for example `_inline_single_pass(manifest)`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The waiver's input is how a model splits ids across findings", "recommendation": "A waiver fires per finding on the ids the reviewer chose to list, and consolidate never checks them against the head run's failing set. Suppose a reviewer files one finding naming only the inherited ids and omits a new failure. Nothing structural catches that. Consolidate could intersect each finding's `failed_tests` with the head tree's run, or refuse a waiver while the head run fails ids that no finding names. This is a design hardening, not a defect shown in this diff.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Weighed as the plan asked: a bare-string failed_tests is refused, not coerced", "recommendation": "hook-surface.md's rule 'When a fail-closed validator guards a model-written field, tolerate the natural encoding variant and hard-fail only on genuine ambiguity' does apply. A bare string is unambiguous, and wrapping it into one element would not shrink the set the waiver must cover, which is the reason given for refusing bad elements. The decision is recorded, though, and it rests on #900's acceptance. Its cost is now small: `validate-partial` catches it before the reviewer returns, and otherwise it costs one reviewer re-dispatch, not the round. Keeping it is defensible as recorded. If it ever trips a real review, coercing str to [str] is the one-line relaxation.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass covered all four oracle files; only one claim cleared a bar", "recommendation": "Covered: .prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md, .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-review-round-cost.md, .prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md, .prawduct/changes/review-round-cost.md. The validate-partial entry (exits 0/1/2, line and column), the data-model resolution and test-run bodies (newest non-narrowed run on the merge-base tree, 100-id cap, `base_run`), and the plan's mid-build decisions all match the code. The one record claim that clears the 'It ships' bar is the Consumer note's 'the branch's next review waives', which is folded into the scope-trace finding as its consequence. The plan's citation of `.prawduct/owner-rulings.md` 'on `feature/gate-clarity`' points at another branch. That is history and misleads no action, so no edit is wanted.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-review-round-cost.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/review-round-cost.md"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The consolidation refusal path is well observed", "recommendation": "`_partial_failure_message` judges each other role from disk through the same `check_partial`, separating passed, pending and also-failing roles, and gives a remedy fitted to the roster. A failed waiver append prints and fails closed, and an idempotent re-run heals it. Nothing to change.", "